"How old are you? That is easy, because someone wrote down the day you were born. How old is a tree? You can count its rings. But how old is the Earth? Nobody was there at the beginning, and there are no rings to count. For a long time people could only guess.": "f69e74e26b72",
"How old are you? That is easy, because someone wrote down the day you were born. How old is a tree? You can count its rings. But how old is the Earth? Nobody was there at the beginning, and there are no rings to count. For a long time people could only guess. The answer turned out to be inside rocks. Everything is made of atoms, and most atoms stay the same forever. But a few kinds are not steady. Sooner or later, an atom of one of these kinds changes into a different kind of atom. Uranium, for example, slowly changes into lead. Nobody can tell when one single atom will change. But a rock holds a huge number of atoms, and for a huge number the pace is perfectly regular. After a certain length of time, half of the atoms will have changed. This length of time is called the half-life. After one more half-life, half of what was left will have changed, so a quarter remains. Heat, cold, and pressure do not change the pace. This gives us a clock. Suppose a rock had uranium in it when it formed, but no lead. Today we measure it and find just as much lead as uranium. Half of the uranium has changed, so one half-life has passed. The half-life of the most common kind of uranium is about 4.47 billion years. This rock is very old indeed. The oldest rocks of the Earth itself have been melted, crushed, and worn away, so scientists looked for something that formed at the same time and has not changed since. They found it in meteorites, pieces of rock from space that formed along with the planets. In 1956, an American scientist named Clair Patterson measured the uranium and lead in meteorites and got an age of about 4.55 billion years. Today the age of the Earth is given as about 4.54 billion years, and other clues agree. The oldest grains of mineral ever found on Earth are about 4.4 billion years old, and the oldest rocks from the Moon are about as old as that too. A number that big is hard to imagine. One way is to squeeze the whole story into one day. On that clock, our own kind of human arrives less than six seconds before midnight.": "691e3dc9e626",
